---
/**
 * The terms of service.
 *
 * Written the way the privacy policy next to it is: as a description of what
 * this software actually does and what we actually promise, so that every
 * clause can be checked against a file in this repository. The disclaimers are
 * the ones a platform for evaluating physical AI agents has to make out loud —
 * an evaluation is evidence, not a certificate, and a system that moves in the
 * world is the operator's responsibility. If the code changes, this changes
 * with it.
 *
 * It shares the prose shell with `privacy.astro`: same measure, same header,
 * same rule. A reader who has read one should not have to learn a second page.
 *
 * Two things it is deliberately careful about. OmniDynamics is new and is not
 * incorporated yet, so nothing here may imply a company that does not exist:
 * there is no entity name, no registration number, no registered office, no ™
 * or ® on the mark, and the agreement is with the people who run it. And the
 * one convention this page cannot follow is the American habit of setting
 * disclaimers in capitals for conspicuousness — the house style reserves
 * uppercase for labels and forbids it as emphasis (DESIGN.md §10), and the
 * section headings here are already set uppercase and tracked, which is where
 * the label belongs.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Logo from "../components/Logo.astro";
import { CAL, CAL_READY, EMAIL, PROVIDER_HOST, ROUTES, SIGN_IN_READY } from "../lib/auth/config";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const UPDATED = "15 September 2026";
const calHost = (() => {
  try {
    return new URL(CAL.origin).host;
  } catch {
    return CAL.origin;
  }
})();
---

<Base
  title="Terms — OmniDynamics"
  description="The terms you use this site and our systems under, and what we do and do not promise."
>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="top">
      <a class="brand clicky" href={withBase("/")}>
        <Logo size={30} />
        <span class="wordmark">OmniDynamics</span>
      </a>
      <nav class="links">
        {SIGN_IN_READY && <a class="clicky" href={ROUTES.account}>Your account</a>}
        <a class="clicky" href={ROUTES.book}>Book a demo</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <h1>Terms</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        What you may do with this site and our systems, what we promise, and
        what we do not.
      </p>
      <p class="stamp">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>

      <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <h2>Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        OmniDynamics is a new venture based in Seattle, Washington. It is not
        incorporated yet — there is no company, no registration number and no
        registered office to give you — so "we" on this page means the people
        who build and run it, and this agreement is with them. If and when it is
        incorporated, the entity takes over this agreement on the same terms and
        this page will say so and carry the date it changed.
      </p>
      <p class="thin">
        Being early is the reason several things below are smaller than they
        would be from a large company: there is no service level, no support
        commitment and no warranty. What there is instead is a person reading
        {" "}<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The agreement</h2>
      <p>
        These terms cover this site, anything you reach from it that we run —
        signing in, booking a demo, and the systems the landing page opens — and
        any evaluation or deployment we do for you that has no separate signed
        contract. Using any of it means you accept them. If you do not, the
        honest thing is to close the tab, and nothing is held either way.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where we have signed something else with you — an order form, a pilot
        agreement, a data processing agreement — that document wins on anything
        it covers, and these terms fill the gaps.
      </p>
      <p class="thin">
        If you are agreeing on behalf of a company, you are saying you are
        allowed to, and "you" means the company from there on.
      </p>

      <h2>Who may use it</h2>
      <p>
        You need to be 16 or older, and legally able to enter an agreement. The
        site is not for children and we do not knowingly collect anything from
        anyone under 16 — see the <a href={ROUTES.privacy}>privacy policy</a>.
        Do not use any of this where doing so would break the law that applies
        to you, including export control and sanctions law.
      </p>

      <h2>Your account</h2>
      {SIGN_IN_READY ? (
        <>
          <p>
            Signing in is <strong>Sign in with Google</strong>, and you
            authenticate at <span class="host">{PROVIDER_HOST}</span> rather
            than here. So the credential is Google's to protect and yours to
            keep: we never see your password, one-time code or passkey, and we
            cannot reset any of them. Keep control of the Google account you
            sign in with, and tell us at
            {" "}<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a> if you lose it.
          </p>
          <p>
            One account is one person. Do not share it, do not sign in as
            somebody else, and do not use an account you are not entitled to.
            You are answerable for what happens under yours.
          </p>
          <p class="thin">
            There is no server here to hold a session, so signing out is a local
            act: the token is discarded from the tab. Your account page says
            exactly what is held and where.
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>
          Sign-in is not enabled on this build of the site, so there is no
          account to hold and nothing in this section applies yet.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
      <p>
        Use this the way it is meant to be used. In particular, do not:
      </p>
      <ul class="who">
        <li>
          <strong>Break in.</strong> No probing, scanning or circumventing
          authentication, rate limits or any other control; no accessing data,
          accounts or systems that are not yours. Security research is welcome
          — write to us first and we will tell you what is in scope.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Break it.</strong> No load intended to degrade the service, no
          automated collection beyond ordinary crawling of the public pages, and
          nothing that interferes with another user.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Take it apart to rebuild it.</strong> No reverse engineering,
          decompiling or extracting our models, weights, prompts, shaders or
          evaluation harness in order to train a competing system or resell
          ours. Reading the client-side source your browser was sent is fine —
          it was sent to you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Feed it what you have no right to.</strong> Do not upload
          anything you do not own or have permission to use, and do not upload
          personal data about other people without a lawful basis for it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Use it against people.</strong> No unlawful, infringing,
          deceptive or harassing use, and nothing intended to harm anyone. We
          do not permit use of our systems to make autonomous decisions about a
          person's safety, rights, employment, credit or liberty.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="thin">
        We may suspend access without notice if something here is being broken
        and the service or another user is at risk, and we will say why as soon
        as we can.
      </p>

      <h2>What you put in</h2>
      <p>
        Your content stays yours. That means your prompts, your recordings, your
        logs, your models, your evaluation data — all of it. You give us the
        narrow permission we need to run what you asked for: to store,
        transmit, process and display it, and to make the copies that running it
        requires. That permission lasts as long as you keep the content with us
        and ends when it is deleted.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not train general-purpose models on your content or hand it to
        anyone else to train theirs. If we ever want to use something of yours
        to improve a system beyond your own deployment, we will ask first and
        you may say no without losing the service.
      </p>
      <p>
        You are responsible for having the rights to what you send, and for
        keeping your own copy of anything you cannot afford to lose.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes out</h2>
      <p>
        Our systems generate things — scripts, scenes, briefings, attention
        fields, evaluation reports. As between us, that output is yours to use.
        Two limits are worth stating plainly, because they are properties of the
        technology and not fine print:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Output can be wrong.</strong> These are probabilistic systems.
        They can be confidently incorrect, and the same input can produce
        different results. Check anything that matters before you rely on it,
        and keep a person in the loop wherever a mistake would cost something.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Output is not unique to you.</strong> A similar prompt from
        somebody else can produce similar output, so we cannot promise your
        results are original or exclusive, and we make no claim that they are
        protectable or free of third-party rights.
      </p>

      <h2>Evaluations and agents</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part specific to what we build. Our evaluation platforms
        record and explain what a physical AI agent attended to and why; an
        evaluation is <em>evidence about a system's behaviour under the
        conditions it was tested in</em>, and nothing more. It is not a
        certification, not a safety case, not regulatory approval, and not a
        licence to deploy.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you operate a drone, a manipulator, a robot or any other system that
        moves in the world, you remain its operator. Site safety, human
        supervision, risk assessment, insurance, airspace and every applicable
        regulation stay with you. Do not use our output as the sole basis for
        letting such a system run unsupervised around people, and do not
        represent a result of ours as approval by anyone.
      </p>

      <h2>What is ours</h2>
      <p>
        The site, our systems, the name, the mark and the wordmark are ours or
        our licensors'. They are unregistered — there is no trademark
        registration behind the name or the monogram, and you will not find a ™
        or an ® anywhere on this site — but they are still ours, and these terms
        give you permission to use the service rather than ownership of any of
        it. Please do not pass off your own work as ours.
      </p>
      <p>
        Parts of the page are other people's work, used under their licences and
        credited in the repository — the glass on the landing page is vendored
        from <a href="https://github.com/vercel-labs/vgpu" rel="noreferrer">vgpu</a>
        {" "}under the MIT licence, with its notice kept alongside the code. Those
        licences govern those parts.
      </p>
      <p class="thin">
        If you think something here infringes your copyright, write to
        {" "}<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a> with enough detail to find
        it and we will look at it and act.
      </p>

      <h2>Other companies</h2>
      <p>
        Three things on this site are run by somebody else, and using them
        brings their terms with it:
      </p>
      <ul class="who">
        {SIGN_IN_READY && (
          <li>
            <strong>Google</strong> — sign-in, under
            {" "}<a href="https://policies.google.com/terms" rel="noreferrer">its terms</a>.
          </li>
        )}
        {CAL_READY && (
          <li>
            <strong>{calHost}</strong> — the booking form and the video room the
            call is held in, under
            {" "}<a href="https://cal.com/terms" rel="noreferrer">Cal.com's terms</a>.
          </li>
        )}
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> — serves these files.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Links out to a system's own site are the same: once you leave, you are
        on their terms, and we are not responsible for what is there.
      </p>

      <h2>Demos and bookings</h2>
      {CAL_READY ? (
        <p>
          A booked call is a conversation, not a contract. Either of us may move
          or cancel it — use the link in the confirmation, or reply to it — and
          nothing said in a demo is a commitment until it is written down
          somewhere both of us signed.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          Booking is not enabled on this build. Mail to
          {" "}<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a> reaches us directly, and
          the same applies: a conversation is not a contract.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2>What it costs</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing, today. This site, signing in and booking a demo are free, and
        there is no payment form anywhere on it. If we introduce a paid plan we
        will say what it costs before you owe anything, and any fees, taxes,
        renewal and refund terms will be set out where you buy it rather than
        buried here.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        What we collect, who processes it and how to get rid of it is on the
        {" "}<a href={ROUTES.privacy}>privacy policy</a>, which is part of these
        terms. It is written next to the code it describes and says the same
        thing this page does: there is very little, and you can see all of it.
      </p>

      <h2>No warranty</h2>
      <p>
        The service is provided as it is and as it is available. To the extent
        the law allows, we disclaim the implied warranties — merchantability,
        fitness for a particular purpose, non-infringement — and we do not
        promise that it will be uninterrupted, error-free, or that any result
        will be accurate, complete or fit for a decision you are about to make.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no uptime commitment and no support commitment, because we are
        not in a position to make one honestly: this is a small, new team, and
        any of it may change, move or be withdrawn. Most of what is here is
        described as early on the page where it appears — three of the five
        systems in the glass have nothing behind them yet but a page saying so.
        The 3D wall needs WebGPU and will not run everywhere; when a browser
        cannot draw it the page says so rather than pretending.
      </p>

      <h2>Liability</h2>
      <p>
        To the extent the law allows, neither of us is liable to the other for
        indirect, incidental, special or consequential loss, or for lost
        profits, revenue, goodwill or data, however it arises. Our total
        liability for any claim connected with the service is capped at what you
        paid us for it in the twelve months before the claim, or US$100 if you
        paid us nothing — which today is everyone, because nothing here costs
        anything.
      </p>
      <p class="thin">
        Nothing here limits liability that cannot lawfully be limited —
        including for death or personal injury caused by our negligence, or for
        fraud or fraudulent misrepresentation — and if you are a consumer, your
        statutory rights are unaffected by anything on this page. Some states
        and countries do not allow these exclusions, and where that is so they
        do not apply to you.
      </p>

      <h2>If you cause a claim</h2>
      <p>
        If someone brings a claim against us because of what you sent us, what
        you did with what came out, or your breach of these terms, you will
        cover the reasonable, direct cost of defending and settling it. We will
        tell you about the claim promptly, let you run the defence with counsel
        of your choosing, and not settle it in a way that binds you without
        asking. This does not apply to a claim that our own act or omission
        caused, and it does not apply at all if you are using the site as a
        consumer rather than for a business.
      </p>

      <h2>Ending it</h2>
      <p>
        You may stop at any time: close the tab, and — where sign-in is enabled
        — sign out and ask us to delete what is left. We may suspend or end
        access if these terms are broken, if the law requires it, or if we
        retire a system, and we will give reasonable notice unless the reason is
        that giving notice would make things worse. The sections that are meant
        to outlive the agreement do: what is ours, the disclaimers, liability,
        your indemnity, and this one.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        This page is versioned alongside the code it describes. Material changes
        move the date at the top, and anything that materially reduces your
        rights will be announced on the site before it takes effect rather than
        applied quietly. Continuing to use the service after a change means you
        accept the new version; if you do not, stop using it.
      </p>

      <h2>Which law applies</h2>
      <p>
        We are in Seattle, so these terms are governed by the law of the State
        of Washington, USA, without regard to its conflict-of-laws rules, and
        any dispute goes to the state or federal courts sitting in King County,
        Washington. Either of us may still use small claims court, wherever it
        is, for something small enough to belong there.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are a consumer outside the United States, none of that takes away
        the mandatory protection of the law where you live, or your right to
        bring a claim in your own courts. And before either of us goes near a
        court, write to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a> and give us
        thirty days: at this size, essentially everything is settled in an
        email.
      </p>
      <p class="thin">
        If a clause here turns out to be unenforceable, the rest stands, and a
        court should read the unenforceable part down rather than throw the page
        out. A right we do not enforce immediately is not a right we have given
        up. You may not transfer this agreement without asking us; we may
        transfer it to the entity that incorporates this venture, or to whoever
        takes the work on, and will say so on this page if we do.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about any of this go to
        {" "}<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>, and are answered by a
        person in Seattle, Washington. There is no postal address to publish
        yet; when there is one, it will be here.
      </p>

      <p class="back"><a class="clicky" href={withBase("/")}>Back to the site</a></p>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The prose shell, the same one `privacy.astro` uses: a 48rem measure on the
     plaster, the header at the top, and no surface of its own anywhere. Kept as
     a copy rather than lifted into a layout because the two pages are the only
     two of their kind and a shared shell for two documents is a shell nobody
     can read — if a third arrives, that is the moment to extract it. */
  .page {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-block-size: 100dvh;
    max-inline-size: 48rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem) clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 2.5rem)
      clamp(3rem, 8vh, 5rem);
    color: var(--on-wall);
  }

  .top {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding-block-end: clamp(2rem, 6vh, 3.5rem);
  }
  .brand {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    border-radius: 999px;
  }
  .wordmark { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .links { display: flex; gap: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2rem); font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  .links a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; opacity: 0.62; transition: opacity 0.2s ease; }
  .links a:hover { opacity: 1; }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 9vw, 5.5rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .lede {
    margin: clamp(0.9rem, 2.5vh, 1.4rem) 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2.2vw, 1.5rem);
    line-height: 1.3;
    font-weight: 300;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .stamp {
    margin: 0.65rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .rule {
    inline-size: min(100%, 22rem);
    block-size: 1px;
    margin-block: clamp(1.5rem, 4vh, 2.5rem);
    background: currentColor;
    opacity: 0.28;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: clamp(2rem, 5vh, 2.75rem) 0 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.62;
    max-inline-size: 38rem;
  }
  p:last-of-type { margin-block-end: 0; }
  .thin { font-size: 0.9375rem; color: var(--muted); }

  a { color: inherit; text-underline-offset: 0.22em; }
  .host, strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }

  /* The one list shape this page needs: a clause per line, its first few words
     carrying the point, at a measure that does not run past the paragraphs. */
  .who {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-inline-size: 38rem;
  }

  .back {
    margin-block-start: clamp(2.5rem, 7vh, 4rem);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
